WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.560
MARK FASHING: Hello, everyone.

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.190
Welcome to Next.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.470
I'm Mark Fashing, software
engineer with the Google Apps

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.440
Script team, here with my
co-presenter, Daniel Florey,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.330
CEO of floreysoft.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.760
And we are here today to
talk to you about something

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.600
we're both really excited
about, building G Suite add-ons

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:22.710
with Google Apps Scripts.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:29.949
So let me give you an idea of
where we're going to go today.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:32.240
First, I'm going to give you
a little bit of background

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.010
on Apps Scripts and
add-ons for those who

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.440
may not be familiar with them.

00:00:36.440 --> 00:00:38.570
Then we'll talk
about how to code

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.120
your own add-on from scratch
and how you would publish it.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:45.030
And then I'm going to hand off
the presentation to Daniel,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:48.120
who's going to show you
the product that he's

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.580
built on our platform,
which I think

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.790
is a really powerful
demonstration of the potential

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.650
of this platform.

00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.450
And he'll do a
live demo as well,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.320
showing how he's used add-ons
to more tightly integrate

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:04.510
with G Suite products.

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:06.970
And then after
that, I'll introduce

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.520
a few of the new features
that we've got planned.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.260
And we'll talk
about what you can

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.440
do to get involved in
publishing add-ons.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:20.800
And then there will be a little
bit of time for Q&amp;A at the end.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:23.280
So first, let me give you
a little bit of background.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.570
What is Apps Script?

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.750
Apps Script is a serverless
JavaScript runtime

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.080
for G Suite automation,
extension, and integration.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.030
That's a bit of a mouthful.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:37.100
But there's a couple
of keywords in here.

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.570
Serverless.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.850
Of course, Apps Script
runs on servers.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:43.560
But the idea is that you
don't have to provision them.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:46.004
So you might call this
zero provisioning.

00:01:46.004 --> 00:01:47.420
And that's pretty
cool, because it

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.961
means you can write
your code and let

00:01:48.961 --> 00:01:51.080
us worry about the rest of it.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:54.490
And the other key word
in here is G Suite.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:57.560
Apps Script is purpose
built for integrating

00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:58.550
with G Suite products.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.580
Which means we have a lot of
built in APIs that you can take

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.385
advantage of that make it
easy to interact with Gmail

00:02:04.385 --> 00:02:09.800
and Calendar and Drive and Docs,
and whatever G Suite products

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.420
you want to interact with with
your script, with your add-on,

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:16.430
with your web app.

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.300
So what are add-ons?

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.480
Add-ons extend G Suite
products so you can get

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.640
the things you need to do done.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.550
And the keyword here is extend.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:33.800
Add-ons run inside G Suite
products, creating menu items,

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:39.260
dialogs, sidebars, so that
you can build functionality

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.150
into that product in
a seamless manner.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.080
For example, let's
say that you would

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:47.240
want Docs to be able
to translate text

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.190
into other languages.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.660
You can build an
add-on for that.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:57.440
One of the unique
things about add-ons

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.380
is that they are a
true cloud technology.

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.400
And people throw around
the word cloud a lot.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.870
You've probably heard it
thrown around a lot here.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:06.880
In the case of add-ons,
it's really true.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.500
Because add-ons are running
in cloud native docs editors,

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.080
and they're running on a
cloud development platform,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.590
it means that for one thing,
they're always up to date.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:22.340
So for example, you've
got a new feature

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.080
that you want to push out
to users in your add-on.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.850
Code it, redeploy,
and the next time

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:33.050
that users use that add-on, the
feature will be there for them.

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:34.280
They don't have to download.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:36.020
They don't have to re-install.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.850
And this is pretty cool.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:42.800
Likewise, domain-wide install
is made easy for enterprise.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.220
If you publish your add-on
to the G Suite marketplace,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.600
domain admins can
install to their domain.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:54.830
And then the next time that
users in their enterprise

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:59.090
use that product, say, Sheets,
that add-on will already

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:00.920
be there for them.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.510
You don't have to
build an installer.

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:04.980
You don't have to push it
out to the workstations.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.882
It's easy to deploy.

00:04:06.882 --> 00:04:08.840
And there are a whole
host of other advantages.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.467
I've listed some of them here.

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:12.800
But I don't have time to talk
through all of them today.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.964
So let's dive right into coding.

00:04:18.964 --> 00:04:21.380
I'm going to show you how to
build a simple Sheets add-on.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:26.150
But first, let me set
the stage for you.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:29.470
So let's say that you're
a Sheets power user.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.010
You really like using Sheets.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.960
But it doesn't have all the
functions that you need.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:37.700
That's OK.

00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:41.000
Apps Script lets you
define your own functions.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.530
We call them custom functions.

00:04:42.530 --> 00:04:44.420
Some people call them
user defined functions.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:45.670
Some people call them macros.

00:04:45.670 --> 00:04:47.961
But you can go to the Script
editor and write your own.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.670
But then it's a bit of a
hassle to copy these around

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.159
from Sheet to Sheet.

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:57.450
You keep having to look back
at your script to remember

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.346
which ones you wrote.

00:04:59.346 --> 00:05:00.720
And now your
teammates are asking

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.120
to use these functions too.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.080
How do we solve this problem?

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:08.040
Let's write an add-on.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:10.260
And to make things a
little more interesting,

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.510
let's add some built-in help.

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:18.510
So you might be familiar with
custom functions already.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:22.120
But basically the idea
is when you're in Sheets

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:26.070
and you need another function,
you can go into the Tools menu

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.550
and click Script Editor.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:31.530
That will take you into the
Apps Script script editor,

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.300
and create a script
for you that's

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.930
embedded in that sheet where
you can write functions

00:05:36.930 --> 00:05:39.690
in ordinary JavaScript,
just like the ones I've

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.920
created here.

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:44.140
These are actual
finance functions

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:49.540
from another popular
spreadsheet software.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:51.040
We don't have them in Sheets.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.100
But with this add-on, now we do.

00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.200
And you can invoke
them in a cell

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.630
just like you would
a built-in function

00:05:58.630 --> 00:06:02.430
with the familiar
equals notation.

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:05.600
So let's get to that help
page I was talking about.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:09.680
So we created these
custom functions.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.629
And I'd like to add some
documentation in a sidebar.

00:06:14.629 --> 00:06:16.170
When you're building
add-ons, there's

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.860
a special method
that Apps Script

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.860
recognizes called onOpen.

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:24.440
We call these methods
simple triggers.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:26.970
But the idea is basically
that if a user has installed

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:30.620
an add-on, and
they open a Sheet,

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:36.890
this onOpen method will get
run when they open a Sheet.

00:06:36.890 --> 00:06:41.030
And this method, as you
can see, adds a menu item,

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:44.940
show documentation, that invokes
this callbacks showSidebar,

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:48.200
this other method we've defined.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:50.480
So that's pretty simple.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.030
And then showSidebar itself
is not very complicated.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:56.840
It just creates a help
page and then opens it

00:06:56.840 --> 00:07:00.500
in a sidebar in the Sheets UI.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:05.390
So what does that
help page look like?

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:07.460
It's just a web page.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.970
So building sidebars is
easy if you're already

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:13.820
familiar with basic web
technologies like HTML, CSS,

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:16.340
and JavaScript.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.020
And you can call
server side functions

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:23.270
in Apps Script from
your sidebar client code

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.670
by using the API
google.script.run.

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:37.110
So in not very much
code, we've done

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.790
something useful in Sheets.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:40.170
We've added some
custom functions

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.720
that we didn't have before.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.850
We've added a menu item
to open our help page,

00:07:44.850 --> 00:07:46.110
and we've added a sidebar.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:49.470
And this is what it actually
looks like inside Sheets

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.040
when we use this add-on.

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:53.440
You can see I'm invoking
the custom function.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:55.930
And there's the documentation
in the sidebar on the right

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:57.930
that I open by clicking the
menu item, which would show up

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:58.830
in the add-ons menu.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:06.860
When you're ready to test
it in the script editor

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:10.670
you can go into the Publish
menu and click Test as add-on.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:13.130
This allows you to simulate
different install states

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.450
on different documents.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:17.367
So you pick sheets that you
want to try your add-on out

00:08:17.367 --> 00:08:22.010
on and select what kind of
installation configuration

00:08:22.010 --> 00:08:23.830
you want.

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:27.020
But remember, it's
OK to make mistakes.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.170
Because like I said at
the beginning of the talk,

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.560
add-ons are always up to date.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:33.570
Which means that if you
have a bug in the add-on

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.960
that you pushed, it's
very low cost to fix it.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.159
Code the fix, republish, and
the next time your users use it,

00:08:41.159 --> 00:08:43.080
they'll get the bug fix.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.910
So feel free to launch and
iterate on a very tight cycle.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:53.510
So now that you've seen how
to code the add-on, let's

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.610
talk about deploying
it and what it

00:08:55.610 --> 00:08:59.530
looks like when a user uses it.

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:03.196
So to deploy the add-on, you
can go into the Publish menu

00:09:03.196 --> 00:09:04.070
in the Script Editor.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:06.610
And this time we're
going to click

00:09:06.610 --> 00:09:09.350
Deploy as Sheets web add-on.

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:13.300
And what happens
then is that we'll

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:17.074
create an entry in the Chrome
Web Store for your add-on.

00:09:17.074 --> 00:09:18.990
And it will give you a
link, you'll follow it,

00:09:18.990 --> 00:09:22.000
and you'll go provide a few
bits of extra information.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.510
Add icons, some promo images,
a description of the add-on.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:31.490
And then you'll decide who
you want to publish it to.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:33.740
You can publish it to the world.

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:35.870
You can publish it
to your business

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:37.830
by publishing to a domain.

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:41.120
You can publish it to a
group of trusted testers.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.390
It's up to you who you want
to share your add-on with.

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:48.620
And then if you want to
allow enterprise users

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.240
to do domain-wide
installs, you can

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:52.282
publish to G Suite marketplace.

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.280
So we made an
add-on, and now users

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:01.292
can find it by
going into Sheets.

00:10:01.292 --> 00:10:02.750
And if you go into
the Add-ons menu

00:10:02.750 --> 00:10:08.940
and click Get Add-ons, something
that you can do right now,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.780
you can browse the
Chrome Web Store

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.060
and search the Chrome
Web Store for add-ons

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:16.590
that people have published.

00:10:16.590 --> 00:10:18.750
In the case of the add-on
that I just demoed,

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.660
I published it to a domain, so
you won't have access to it.

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:23.310
But there are hundreds
of other add-ons

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.590
that have been
published, including

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.638
add-ons that my co-presenter
Daniel has published.

00:10:32.850 --> 00:10:36.120
When you install an add-on,
you'll almost certainly

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:38.100
see this prompt.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:44.390
And I think it's important to
know what this prompt means.

00:10:44.390 --> 00:10:51.540
Apps Script will automatically
determine which permissions

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.030
the script requires to run.

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:57.270
And so if you're add-on
accesses a user's Gmail,

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:00.720
or accesses their email address,
or accesses their Sheets,

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.840
different permissions will
be displayed in this prompt.

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:06.330
And this prompt
will ask the user

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.380
for exactly the permissions
that your add-on needs.

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:10.860
This is all managed for you.

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:13.770
If you've heard
of OAuth 2.0, it's

00:11:13.770 --> 00:11:16.230
an industry standard
security model.

00:11:16.230 --> 00:11:17.989
That's what Apps
Script is using.

00:11:17.989 --> 00:11:19.530
But you don't have
to worry about it,

00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:21.580
because it's built-in.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.060
And that means that users
can trust your add-on

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:28.200
to only access the data that
you've requested permission

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:30.205
that they've
explicitly authorized.

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:37.360
So I'd like to walk briefly
through what it looks like when

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:38.740
a user installs an add-on.

00:11:41.710 --> 00:11:44.380
So let's say user Alice goes
to their Chrome Web Store.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:47.426
And she gets an
add-on from the store.

00:11:47.426 --> 00:11:50.360
Well, now let's say she
installs a Sheets add-on.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:54.830
When she opens a
sheet, now the add-on

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:58.130
will run in a very
limited mode that

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:02.170
basically exists to be able to
add menu items inside Sheets.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:08.890
When she uses the
add-on, the add-on

00:12:08.890 --> 00:12:11.200
will run with all the
privileges that she authorized

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:12.450
when she installed the add-on.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:17.405
But another interesting thing
will happen to that document.

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:26.670
Now triggers like onOpen
and onEdit and onInstall

00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:29.110
will run with
additional privileges.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:31.420
But more importantly,
when user Bob,

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:35.260
who's never installed this
add-on opens the sheet,

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.480
the onOpen trigger
will also run.

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.240
And this is an important
feature for collaborators.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:42.760
Because if Alice has used
this add-on in the document,

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:44.350
chances are Bob wants to too.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.550
And when Bob goes to use
the add-on by selecting

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.050
one of these menu
items, that's when

00:12:51.050 --> 00:12:55.040
Bob will be prompted to install
the add-on from the Chrome Web

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:55.720
Store.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:58.700
And at that time, Bob will
authorize the access required

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.250
for that add-on to run as Bob.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:02.840
This is an important
way for users

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:06.230
to discover your add-ons
when collaborators

00:13:06.230 --> 00:13:07.650
on their documents
have used them.

00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:15.600
So now you've seen how to build
a relatively simple add-on.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.570
I want to give you an impression
of what the true potential

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.280
of this platform is.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:21.780
And I can't think
of a better person

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.740
to do this than my
co-presenter, Daniel Florey, CEO

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:29.250
of floreysoft, who's built his
product Ultradox on the Apps

00:13:29.250 --> 00:13:31.770
Developer Platform,
and uses add-ons

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.365
to more tightly integrate
with G Suite products.

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:37.680
DANIEL FLOREY: OK.

00:13:37.680 --> 00:13:38.490
Thank you, Mark.

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:40.260
And thanks for the
opportunity to show

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.650
Ultradox and the Ultradox
add-ons here at Next.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:48.280
So for all of you who have
never heard of Ultradox before,

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:50.730
let me give you a very
brief idea of what it is

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.320
and why we made it.

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:58.200
So we've created
Ultradox to enable people

00:13:58.200 --> 00:14:02.880
with or without coding skills
to automate their business

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:05.550
and create enterprise apps.

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:07.561
So can we go to the slides?

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:17.040
So Ultradox comes with a set
of high level building blocks.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.790
And you can create
automations or apps

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.150
by assembling these building
blocks using our drag and drop

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.300
interface.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:29.890
And as most enterprise related
business processes also

00:14:29.890 --> 00:14:33.760
involve documents and
emails, Ultradox also

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:37.660
comes with a very
powerful template engine

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:41.350
that you can use to generate
PDF, [INAUDIBLE], Office

00:14:41.350 --> 00:14:44.050
documents right in
the cloud, and also

00:14:44.050 --> 00:14:45.855
to create responsive emails.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.860
So once you're happy
with your flow,

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:53.640
you can then publish your
app with a single click

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:57.750
and use it right away on your
browser, on your mobile device,

00:14:57.750 --> 00:14:59.700
or your desktop.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:05.410
So I think this combination
of features really works.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.280
So we have a large
number of customers

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:12.120
from universities,
schools, and for example,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.540
law firms, who are able to
automate their business.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:19.330
Which is not very surprising,
because the brain of a lawyer

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:22.320
seems to be quite similar
to the brain of a developer.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.100
So they all also like
to think in rules.

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.080
But we also have a
number of customers

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.270
that you would not
expect in the first place

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.340
to create apps by themselves.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:37.960
So for example, like this review
from the G Suite marketplace.

00:15:37.960 --> 00:15:39.880
"As I am a tree surgeon,
my computer skills

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:40.570
are not amazing.

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:43.070
I'm much better with a chainsaw.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:45.490
Ultradox has enabled me
to create my own quoting

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:46.360
machine," and so on.

00:15:46.360 --> 00:15:49.000
So I think this gives
a good impression

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.070
of the variety of customers.

00:15:53.721 --> 00:15:54.220
OK.

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:57.610
So Ultradox comes with a
number of bundled add-ons.

00:15:57.610 --> 00:16:00.610
You can see that in
the Works With section

00:16:00.610 --> 00:16:03.132
when going to the G
Suite marketplace.

00:16:03.132 --> 00:16:04.090
And that's pretty nice.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:07.390
Because once you install
Ultradox from the G Suite

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:10.450
marketplace, you get
all the bundled add-ons

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:12.340
installed right away.

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:16.480
And this installation
flow works fine

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:18.760
no matter if you're using
your personal Gmail account

00:16:18.760 --> 00:16:22.130
or if you are adding it to your
G Suite domain as an admin.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.600
So Ultradox comes with add-ons
for Docs, Sheets, and Forms.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:34.970
And I will show you our Docs
and Forms add-ons in a minute.

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.470
When installing
Ultradox, you also

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:40.160
get a dedicated file
type on Google Drive.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:43.550
So you can create, edit,
and share Ultradox files

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:46.235
on Google Drive just like
the native file types.

00:16:48.900 --> 00:16:51.950
And I think this tight
integration and seamless

00:16:51.950 --> 00:16:55.305
integration is what really sets
the G Suite ecosystem apart

00:16:55.305 --> 00:16:56.180
from the competition.

00:16:56.180 --> 00:16:59.630
Because you can really create
third party applications

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:03.820
that work almost in the same
way as the standard G Suite

00:17:03.820 --> 00:17:04.995
applications.

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:11.660
So now it's time to pray
to the demo-god, because I

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:14.630
want to try to create an
application from scratch,

00:17:14.630 --> 00:17:15.349
live on stage.

00:17:15.349 --> 00:17:19.640
And I would like you
to go to this URL

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:23.109
because that should take
you to a Google form.

00:17:23.109 --> 00:17:26.390
And I will try to create
an automation that will get

00:17:26.390 --> 00:17:30.500
triggered by your Google form.

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:32.810
And then we'll create a
personalized PDF document

00:17:32.810 --> 00:17:35.090
and send it as an
attachment to you by email.

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.610
So just open the Google form.

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:43.130
Don't submit it right
now because it will not

00:17:43.130 --> 00:17:45.000
do anything useful yet.

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:49.340
But we will use that
in 10 or 15 minutes.

00:17:58.351 --> 00:17:58.850
OK.

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:05.130
So hopefully, some of you
have the form open already.

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:05.630
OK.

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:13.390
Otherwise, I ask the guys
next to you for the URL.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:13.890
OK.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:16.300
So can we switch to
the demo machine?

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:23.690
So when logging
into Ultradox, you

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:27.260
will find a number of
pre-built solutions,

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:30.290
similar to what you see when
logging into Google Docs.

00:18:30.290 --> 00:18:33.950
But each of these solutions
comes with a number of files.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:38.790
So when using these examples
as a starting point,

00:18:38.790 --> 00:18:42.110
you will then get a folder on
your Google Drive containing

00:18:42.110 --> 00:18:43.460
all the involved files.

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:47.800
So we have readymade solutions
from simple newsletter sign

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:51.830
up systems, generating
invoices from a timesheet.

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:55.970
Up to more complete applications
like our famous PDF invoicing

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.190
for Stripe that you can
add to your Stripe account

00:18:58.190 --> 00:19:03.260
to automatically send out nice
PDF invoices to your customers,

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:09.230
or complete approval flows like
the purchase order example.

00:19:09.230 --> 00:19:14.330
But for this demo, I will just
start with a blank document.

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:16.640
So you can also create
a blank document

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:18.320
from the Google Drive New menu.

00:19:22.650 --> 00:19:24.930
So when opening a new
document, a wizard

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:27.840
will pop up to guide you through
the most common use cases.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.600
But in this example,
as I hopefully

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:32.940
should know what I'm doing,
I will just get rid of that

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:33.898
and start from scratch.

00:19:38.210 --> 00:19:41.540
First of all, I'll
give our file a name.

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:44.990
So that when switching
to Google Drive,

00:19:44.990 --> 00:19:49.310
you will then find
the corresponding file

00:19:49.310 --> 00:19:52.905
with an Ultradox
file icon over here.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:56.600
OK.

00:19:56.600 --> 00:20:01.220
So let's, first of all,
have a look at the editor.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.170
On the left hand
side, you will find

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:05.150
a number of building
blocks that you

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:06.800
can use to create
your application,

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:10.190
from very simple stuff like
uploading, downloading files

00:20:10.190 --> 00:20:13.160
from Google Drive, to
more complex building

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:16.520
blocks, for example, a complete
check out flow that you can add

00:20:16.520 --> 00:20:19.040
to your application by
simply dragging that building

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:20.990
block to your flow.

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:25.020
But as I said, I wanted to
start with a Google form.

00:20:25.020 --> 00:20:27.140
I will use this building block.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:29.870
And when creating a
building block that

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:31.790
is referencing an
external file, you

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.460
can either create a
new file from scratch

00:20:34.460 --> 00:20:36.740
or select an existing
one from Google Drive.

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:40.190
So I will pick the one
that I've prepared.

00:20:40.190 --> 00:20:42.350
I think that's the
one that you hopefully

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.265
have open on your
mobile device or laptop.

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:49.130
So each building block
comes with some input

00:20:49.130 --> 00:20:52.530
on the left hand side and
output on the right hand side.

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:57.440
So I will add some
dummy data so that I

00:20:57.440 --> 00:21:02.600
can test my flow without
submitting the form each time.

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:14.230
So next, I want to
generate a PDF document

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:15.610
from a Google document.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:19.215
So I will drag this Google
Docs to PDF document building

00:21:19.215 --> 00:21:21.310
block to the flow.

00:21:21.310 --> 00:21:24.550
And I will select
a Google document

00:21:24.550 --> 00:21:25.960
that I've prepared
for this demo.

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:32.850
So when going to
Google Docs, this

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:37.680
will open just a
plain Google document.

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:44.460
And in Google Docs, you will
now find the Ultradox add-ons.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:48.090
And I will get started with
the Ultradox template editor.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:51.630
So this one has been
designed to help you

00:21:51.630 --> 00:21:55.860
when working on documents
that end up as a PDF or Word

00:21:55.860 --> 00:21:57.330
document.

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:02.070
So in the sidebar, you
will now find all variables

00:22:02.070 --> 00:22:04.920
from the building blocks that
you have added to your flow.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:09.100
In our example, we just have the
variables from the Google Form.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.640
So if I want to
personalize this document,

00:22:11.640 --> 00:22:16.060
I can just go here and
click on the name variable.

00:22:16.060 --> 00:22:18.720
And this will bring up a
bunch of formatting options,

00:22:18.720 --> 00:22:22.230
like printing the string
in uppercase letters only

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:23.830
or stuff like that.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:27.530
But I will just
insert it as it is.

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:32.810
And I can also add the
time stamp as a date.

00:22:32.810 --> 00:22:37.940
And as Ultradox has detected
that the response time stamp is

00:22:37.940 --> 00:22:41.750
of type date, you will now get a
lot of date formatting options.

00:22:41.750 --> 00:22:47.000
So this is probably not very
important in the United States.

00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:49.430
But if you are
sending out invoices

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.670
in Europe or across
the world, then it's

00:22:52.670 --> 00:22:55.430
really the tricky part to get
all the date, currency, number

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:56.450
formatting right.

00:22:56.450 --> 00:23:01.200
So Ultradox also
helps you with that.

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:04.400
But aside from these
simple merge tags,

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:08.630
you can also use more complex
stuff in your templates

00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:10.410
like loops or conditions.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:15.380
So I have created a table
where certain table rows will

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:19.610
be then visible
depending on the role

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.050
that you are picking
in your form.

00:23:22.050 --> 00:23:24.770
So when clicking on
the preview icon,

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:30.770
we should now get a PDF
preview using the dummy data

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:35.420
that I've entered into
my form and with the text

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:36.800
block for the picked role.

00:23:39.440 --> 00:23:39.940
OK.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:43.180
So next, I want
to create an email

00:23:43.180 --> 00:23:47.380
that I want to send out
with this document attached.

00:23:47.380 --> 00:23:50.440
So as I want to use
a nice HTML email,

00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:54.100
I will use the Google Docs
to HTML building block.

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:57.005
And this time I will start
with a blank document.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:02.190
So let's give that a name.

00:24:12.210 --> 00:24:16.040
And we can all check out
the Email Designer add-on.

00:24:16.040 --> 00:24:18.590
So our Email
Designer add-on comes

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:21.880
with a bunch of
predefined layouts

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.560
that play nicely on
desktop and mobile devices.

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:29.120
So that's always the tricky part
when sending out HTML emails.

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:34.760
So I'll get started with
a very simple theme.

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:39.140
And the basic concept is
that you assemble your emails

00:24:39.140 --> 00:24:41.370
from predefined modules.

00:24:41.370 --> 00:24:43.970
So you will find a number
of matching modules

00:24:43.970 --> 00:24:45.620
in the sidebar.

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:49.640
And some modules
have already been

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:52.320
assembled as a starting point.

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:54.770
So this is just an
ordinary Google document.

00:24:54.770 --> 00:25:00.385
So I can go ahead and work on it
just like you know it already.

00:25:10.870 --> 00:25:13.410
I can get rid of some
columns that I don't need.

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:18.300
And for this demo,
now I want to show

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:21.200
you a more advanced
feature of Ultradox.

00:25:24.680 --> 00:25:28.580
You cannot only insert very
simple data like the name

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:32.120
and timestamp that we've seen,
but Ultradox is also able

00:25:32.120 --> 00:25:34.520
to deal with complex data.

00:25:34.520 --> 00:25:39.920
So as an example, let's load
some data from a Google sheet.

00:25:39.920 --> 00:25:43.520
So I've prepared a sheet
with some of the recommended

00:25:43.520 --> 00:25:45.980
sessions of the next days.

00:25:45.980 --> 00:25:52.730
And now you will see all
the available columns

00:25:52.730 --> 00:25:55.580
in the sheet as area
variables on the right hand

00:25:55.580 --> 00:25:56.810
side of the building block.

00:25:56.810 --> 00:26:01.470
So this is the simple
sheet that I've prepared.

00:26:01.470 --> 00:26:04.580
And these are the corresponding
variables that you will now

00:26:04.580 --> 00:26:07.340
get in Ultradox.

00:26:07.340 --> 00:26:10.250
So in the Email
Designer, you will also

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:14.840
find the Variables tab where
you can insert variables

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:18.080
that you have been loading
before in your flow.

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:25.550
So now I can go ahead and insert
some variables that I've just

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.200
loaded from the Google sheet.

00:26:27.200 --> 00:26:32.540
For example, title and
description of the session.

00:26:32.540 --> 00:26:37.250
And I can also do
more fancy stuff

00:26:37.250 --> 00:26:41.190
like rendering this image
to your edit as an image.

00:26:41.190 --> 00:26:46.596
So I can now specify some
image-specific formatting

00:26:46.596 --> 00:26:47.095
options.

00:26:57.850 --> 00:27:00.820
And the nice thing
is Ultradox knows

00:27:00.820 --> 00:27:02.770
that these variables are areas.

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:05.120
So they contain multiple values.

00:27:05.120 --> 00:27:08.890
So when generating a
preview of this email,

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:14.140
Ultradox should now
automatically expand

00:27:14.140 --> 00:27:18.850
the variables from the
sheet and create an entry

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:20.740
for each row in the sheet.

00:27:25.990 --> 00:27:26.890
OK.

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:27.640
Took a while.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:33.950
So you will see an entry
for each row in the sheet.

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:38.200
So the row in the email has
been repeated automatically.

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:42.520
So that's pretty nice when
dealing with more complex data

00:27:42.520 --> 00:27:43.460
structures.

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:46.510
So for example, when
creating invoices,

00:27:46.510 --> 00:27:53.080
this is always the
tricky part to have

00:27:53.080 --> 00:27:57.100
to display complex data.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:58.950
On the right hand
side, you will find

00:27:58.950 --> 00:28:00.505
a preview for mobile devices.

00:28:00.505 --> 00:28:01.880
And on the left
hand side, you'll

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:04.315
see how this will
look like on desktops.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:08.420
OK.

00:28:08.420 --> 00:28:12.889
Let me just switch to a
nicer version of this email

00:28:12.889 --> 00:28:13.680
that I've prepared.

00:28:18.400 --> 00:28:22.860
And finally, I want to
send out this email--

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.710
this HTML document as an email.

00:28:24.710 --> 00:28:28.240
So we can pick the
sender address.

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:32.440
And Ultradox provides
various email providers,

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:35.920
so you can send out
using your Gmail account,

00:28:35.920 --> 00:28:39.820
or you can also use
Amazon's own SES.

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:43.350
That allows you to track your
email deliveries and bounces.

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:56.110
So I want to attach the
PDF document that we've

00:28:56.110 --> 00:28:57.470
generated previously.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:10.970
And I will also try to track
these emails so I could either

00:29:10.970 --> 00:29:14.990
trigger certain actions whenever
the email gets delivered

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:16.010
or opened.

00:29:16.010 --> 00:29:19.280
But I just want to use Google
Analytics to track the email

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:20.780
deliveries.

00:29:20.780 --> 00:29:22.715
So I will have to
copy and paste.

00:29:31.080 --> 00:29:33.260
And finally, as this
is a cloud conference,

00:29:33.260 --> 00:29:36.330
and I want to push the
cloud to its limits,

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.900
I will also add a Google
Cloud Print building block.

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:44.070
So this will print
the PDF document

00:29:44.070 --> 00:29:48.570
that we are generating right
from the cloud on the printer

00:29:48.570 --> 00:29:50.050
in my office back in Germany.

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:53.820
So this is a very nice feature
because if you, for example,

00:29:53.820 --> 00:29:56.030
are generating invoices,
you have an online shop

00:29:56.030 --> 00:29:59.130
and you are triggering
the invoice generation,

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:01.380
then you could, for
example, automatically

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:04.140
print out the invoices in
the office of your accountant

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:05.440
and stuff like that.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:11.121
So I think that's pretty
nice with Google Cloud Print.

00:30:11.121 --> 00:30:11.620
OK.

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:19.260
Almost forgot to switch on the
trigger in the Google form.

00:30:19.260 --> 00:30:23.030
So in Google Forms, you will
find the Ultradox trigger

00:30:23.030 --> 00:30:24.170
add-on.

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:27.620
And this one will
allow you to trigger

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.778
the flow that we've created
whenever a form gets submitted.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:38.370
So we should activate
it over here.

00:30:38.370 --> 00:30:43.740
And you will also be able
to rename the variable.

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:46.410
So by default they are
based on the question type,

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:48.200
so that's fine with me.

00:30:51.110 --> 00:30:51.610
OK.

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:04.540
Somebody already submitted
the form, I guess.

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:05.510
Let's check.

00:31:05.510 --> 00:31:06.010
OK.

00:31:06.010 --> 00:31:08.040
You can also give it a try.

00:31:08.040 --> 00:31:10.750
And the nice thing
is that you can

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:15.885
see in real time in Ultradox if
anybody is submitting the form.

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:37.220
So I should receive an
email with the attachment.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:41.510
And we can also go to
Google Analytics and check--

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:48.810
somebody did not enter
the correct email.

00:31:48.810 --> 00:31:58.750
But at least-- this is the
email that I've just received.

00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:03.540
And attached I will find the
personalized PDF document.

00:32:03.540 --> 00:32:09.170
So let's finally check
if the printing works.

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:19.770
So I asked my sister to look--

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:20.400
OK.

00:32:20.400 --> 00:32:22.850
Because it's midnight
now in Germany.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:25.660
So something is going on there.

00:32:37.160 --> 00:32:37.660
OK.

00:32:37.660 --> 00:32:39.700
So that's all from my end.

00:32:39.700 --> 00:32:41.730
So back to you.

00:32:41.730 --> 00:32:43.780
MARK FASHING: Daniel,
I think that's a pretty

00:32:43.780 --> 00:32:48.520
cool presentation of what
you can build with add-ons.

00:32:48.520 --> 00:32:50.980
Daniel showed you both a
Docs and a Forms add-on.

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:54.124
So now you've seen the whole
space covered-- and Sheets.

00:32:54.124 --> 00:32:56.290
Well, you think you've seen
the whole space covered.

00:32:59.790 --> 00:33:01.710
Can we-- oh, we
are back on slides.

00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:02.430
Awesome.

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:03.888
So I'm going to
tell you about some

00:33:03.888 --> 00:33:06.370
of the new exciting features
that we've got in the works.

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:14.040
So first off, mobile add-ons
have been out for a while.

00:33:14.040 --> 00:33:16.650
These aren't really new
anymore, but we have

00:33:16.650 --> 00:33:19.350
support for Docs and Sheets.

00:33:19.350 --> 00:33:21.420
And the cool thing
about mobile add-ons

00:33:21.420 --> 00:33:24.000
is that they are
native Android apps.

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:26.929
Which means if you already
have an Android app,

00:33:26.929 --> 00:33:28.470
you can make it into
a mobile add-on.

00:33:31.600 --> 00:33:36.580
And you can also invoke server
side functions in Apps Script

00:33:36.580 --> 00:33:39.670
by using the execution API.

00:33:39.670 --> 00:33:41.750
This code is an example
of how you would do that.

00:33:41.750 --> 00:33:42.920
But it's relatively simple.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:47.650
You just create a request and
build this operation type.

00:33:47.650 --> 00:33:53.200
And then you can send it to
Apps Script using this API.

00:33:53.200 --> 00:33:54.850
And it will execute
asynchronously.

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:56.950
And you can get the
results when you're ready.

00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:03.320
So a good example
of a mobile add-on

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:06.050
is EasyBib built
to mobile add-on.

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:09.690
They already had a mobile app.

00:34:09.690 --> 00:34:13.580
And what their add-on does
is it integrates with Docs.

00:34:13.580 --> 00:34:18.330
So you can go into the
add-ons menu in Docs,

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:22.830
you can select the EasyBib app.

00:34:22.830 --> 00:34:27.210
And that will take you
into their app, where

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:29.820
you can search for the
citation that you're

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:32.500
looking for for your paper.

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:37.980
And you can add it
to your bibliography

00:34:37.980 --> 00:34:39.570
and say to cite it in the Doc.

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:44.190
That will cause that entry to
get inserted into your Doc.

00:34:44.190 --> 00:34:47.550
And you'll be back in the
Doc like you never left.

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:50.670
And so this creates a
much tighter integration,

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:53.400
where you don't have to
jump between mobile apps.

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:57.392
It's all handled by
the add-on developer.

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:04.550
So new features-- you might
have heard an announcement

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.720
this morning from ProvOKer.

00:35:07.720 --> 00:35:09.370
Gmail add-ons are on the way.

00:35:11.985 --> 00:35:13.610
We're really excited
about this launch.

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:15.110
We think that people
are going to do

00:35:15.110 --> 00:35:16.556
really cool things with Gmail.

00:35:16.556 --> 00:35:17.930
And if you want
to find out more,

00:35:17.930 --> 00:35:19.763
if you want to be one
of those people that's

00:35:19.763 --> 00:35:22.250
doing really cool
things with Gmail,

00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:25.910
we have a session at Next that
I encourage you to attend.

00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:30.541
It's right here in the Marriott
in Salon 6 at 5:20 today.

00:35:30.541 --> 00:35:32.840
The title of the
talk is "A new way

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:34.490
to integrate your
app into Gmail."

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:43.360
Another launch that
we've got coming up--

00:35:43.360 --> 00:35:46.310
Slides add-ons.

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:51.200
It's also going to be a
really cool new feature.

00:35:51.200 --> 00:35:54.860
And you have the opportunity
to sign up for the early access

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:57.020
program now.

00:35:57.020 --> 00:36:00.060
You can go to this
link to sign up

00:36:00.060 --> 00:36:02.930
for the early access program.

00:36:02.930 --> 00:36:04.880
I'll give you a second
to copy it down.

00:36:04.880 --> 00:36:07.730
Or you can ask me after I
talk and I'll give it to you.

00:36:07.730 --> 00:36:11.230
And then for more information,
also feel free to visit

00:36:11.230 --> 00:36:15.890
developers.google.com/slides,
where I have been promised

00:36:15.890 --> 00:36:17.750
there will be more
info on slides add-ons.

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:24.430
Everybody who wanted to get an
opportunity to copy this down?

00:36:27.250 --> 00:36:29.380
All right, awesome.

00:36:29.380 --> 00:36:31.510
So what next?

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:33.340
Where do we go from here?

00:36:33.340 --> 00:36:36.180
Well, first of all,
I invite you to go

00:36:36.180 --> 00:36:38.520
to developers.google.com/apps
script/.

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:44.529
This is a great resource our
developer relations team--

00:36:44.529 --> 00:36:46.320
some of whom are actually
in the audience--

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:49.050
have done a really awesome
job of putting together

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:53.520
quickstart guides, tutorials,
reference documents,

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:56.550
and information about
our early access program.

00:36:56.550 --> 00:36:58.230
The quickstart
guides in particular

00:36:58.230 --> 00:37:00.480
are of interest if you're
interested in add-ons.

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:03.240
Because we have a quickstart
guide for Docs add-ons

00:37:03.240 --> 00:37:05.190
and for Forms add-ons.

00:37:05.190 --> 00:37:07.620
They only take about five
minutes to go through.

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:09.480
They're incredibly informative.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:11.197
I've done them myself.

00:37:11.197 --> 00:37:13.030
They're very useful,
and you'll learn a lot.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:19.020
Also, I encourage you
to watch more YouTube--

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:20.260
just in general.

00:37:20.260 --> 00:37:23.910
But if you want to
learn about Apps Script,

00:37:23.910 --> 00:37:29.820
we've got some really
great tutorials online

00:37:29.820 --> 00:37:31.650
on the Apps Scripts platform.

00:37:31.650 --> 00:37:36.824
Unfortunately, we don't
have a talk yet on add-ons.

00:37:36.824 --> 00:37:38.490
Although, Wesley, I
hope you'll add one.

00:37:44.460 --> 00:37:46.260
But these are a
really great resource.

00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:47.730
If you're just getting
started with Apps Script,

00:37:47.730 --> 00:37:49.313
you can learn a lot
from these videos.

00:37:51.770 --> 00:37:55.284
And then since you're
at Next, I encourage

00:37:55.284 --> 00:37:56.950
you to take advantage
of the opportunity

00:37:56.950 --> 00:38:00.700
to attend more Next
sessions on this topic.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:02.680
In particular,
we're going to have

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:06.190
a talk on how to use Stackdriver
logging and BigQuery to do

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:08.140
monitoring in analytics.

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:10.510
This is going to be
really interesting.

00:38:10.510 --> 00:38:14.500
And it's a rare opportunity
to get insight into how

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:17.180
to leverage these technologies.

00:38:17.180 --> 00:38:20.410
And I think it could be very
useful to add-on developers,

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:23.540
because a successful add-on can
easily have tens of thousands

00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:24.040
of users.

00:38:27.170 --> 00:38:29.870
That's going to take
place in Moscone West,

00:38:29.870 --> 00:38:34.040
in room 2010 at 4 o'clock,
which is in the next Breakout

00:38:34.040 --> 00:38:35.642
session.

00:38:35.642 --> 00:38:37.100
But you're going
to have to choose.

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:41.780
Because there is also a
talk on Docs APIs and Apps

00:38:41.780 --> 00:38:46.820
Script taking place here
in the Marriott in Salon 7

00:38:46.820 --> 00:38:49.220
also at 4:00 PM.

00:38:49.220 --> 00:38:52.040
And it's also incredibly
useful for people interested

00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:54.870
in add-ons, because
this talk is going

00:38:54.870 --> 00:38:56.870
to help you learn how to
integrate with the Docs

00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:59.520
editors.

00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:01.470
And finally, as I
mentioned, there's

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:03.900
going to be a session
on Gmail add-ons

00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:06.690
here in the Marriott
at 5:20 in Salon 6.

00:39:11.040 --> 00:39:14.370
So with that, I'd
like to invite you all

00:39:14.370 --> 00:39:16.620
to publish your own add-on.

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:20.280
Think about what
you need, build it,

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.690
decide who you want
to share it with.

00:39:22.690 --> 00:39:25.590
You can publish it
to the world, or you

00:39:25.590 --> 00:39:30.230
can publish it to your business,
but build something cool.

00:39:30.230 --> 00:39:33.290
And when you have
published it, know

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:35.960
that it will be easy
for users to install.

00:39:35.960 --> 00:39:39.430
Whether they want to install
it one user at a time,

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:43.980
or whether businesses want to
install it to their domain.

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:48.110
And remember that we have
this tight launch and iterate

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:52.120
cycle that's made possible
by being a cloud platform.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:54.820
And take advantage
of that so that you

00:39:54.820 --> 00:39:56.090
can publish early and often.

00:39:59.910 --> 00:40:02.790
And remember that because
you're building on Apps Script,

00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:06.060
it's going to be easy to
integrate with Google services,

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:08.730
like Gmail and
Calendar and Drive.

00:40:08.730 --> 00:40:11.430
You can do lots of
cool things this way.

00:40:11.430 --> 00:40:14.370
And we'll take care of
a lot of the details,

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:19.140
like the OAuth 2.0
security model,

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:22.410
and make it easy for you to make
a rich experience in the Google

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:24.510
ecosystem.

00:40:24.510 --> 00:40:28.160
[MUSIC PLAYING]

